      "math_academy_id": "q-184129",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=5-6k,$ where $k\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument showing that $n$ is odd.\n\nThe number $n$ is odd since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =5-6k \\\\  & =2({{field-1}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=$ {{field-3}}, which is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is odd since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =5-6k \\\\  & =(-6k+4)+1 \\\\  & =2({{-3k+2}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=-3k+2,$ which is an integer.",
